<!-- Nomad vs Saily Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set nomadBest = providerA.codes | findCode("NOMTRAVEL20") %}
  {% set sailyPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you're traveling or living abroad for months at a time and want a data bundle that doesn't expire on the usual 30-day clock. Pick <strong>Saily</strong> if you're bouncing between several countries on one trip and would rather have a security suite and ad blocker built in than hunt for a VPN app separately. Both are per-GB pricing underneath, but they're built for different trip shapes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Long-Term Bundles vs. One Universal eSIM</h2>
  <p>
    Nomad's standard plans work like most other per-GB eSIMs on this site: pick a country, region, or global bundle, pay, scan, go. What sets it apart is a second product line called <strong>Global-EX</strong>, bigger 10GB to 50GB bundles that stay valid for up to a year instead of the usual 7 to 30 days, with the per-GB rate dropping to roughly $2.58/GB on the largest bundles. Nothing in Saily's lineup matches that; its plans run on the same kind of short validity windows as most other providers here.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saily's structural difference is different: it sells one universal eSIM rather than a separate profile per country or region. Once it's installed, it keeps working as you cross into a new supported country, no reinstalling or swapping eSIMs at each border. Nomad requires picking a new country, region, or global plan as your trip changes, though a Nomad plan's data can be shared across multiple devices at once, which isn't guaranteed on every provider on this site.
  </p>

